/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Exodus chapters 6-10
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("exodus", [
{n:6, name:"I Will Bring You Out", seal:{ref:"Exodus 6:6",verse:"Wherefore say unto the children of Israel, I am the LORD, and I will bring you out from under the burdens of the Egyptians, and I will rid you out of their bondage, and I will redeem you with a stretched out arm, and with great judgments:"}, facts:["Did you know God says in Exodus 6:3 that He appeared to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob as God Almighty, but by the name JEHOVAH He was not yet known to them? That is the covenant name Moses was sent to carry into Egypt.","Did you know Exodus 6 traces Moses and Aaron back through Levi, Kohath, and Amram, and names their mother Jochebed, the daughter of Levi? The deliverers came from an ordinary family line, recorded with care.","Did you know the Israelites could not even listen to Moses' promise of freedom because of anguish of spirit and cruel bondage (Exodus 6:9)? Long suffering can deafen hope, which is why God spoke to Moses first."], questions:[
{q:"In Exodus 6:2, how does God first identify Himself to Moses?", options:["I am the LORD","I am a strong tower","I am the God of war","I am the Ancient of Days"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:2",verse:"And God spake unto Moses, and said unto him, I am the LORD:", insight:"Before any promise or command, God grounds Moses in who He is. When you know the I AM sends you, the mission stops depending on you."},
{q:"What name does God say He is making known in Exodus 6:3?", options:["JEHOVAH","El Shaddai","Most High","Ancient of Days"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:3",verse:"And I appeared unto Abraham, unto Isaac, and unto Jacob, by the name of God Almighty, but by my name JEHOVAH was I not known to them.", insight:"The patriarchs knew God as God Almighty; now Moses receives the covenant name JEHOVAH. God reveals more of Himself as His plan moves forward."},
{q:"What did God say He had established with the patriarchs in Exodus 6:4?", options:["His covenant, to give them the land of Canaan","A yearly tribute of gold","A temple on Mount Bethel","A lasting peace with Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:4",verse:"And I have also established my covenant with them, to give them the land of Canaan, the land of their pilgrimage, wherein they were strangers.", insight:"God's rescue of Israel was not a new idea but an old promise kept. What He covenants, He completes."},
{q:"Whose groaning did God hear, according to Exodus 6:5?", options:["The children of Israel","The Egyptians","The Midianites","The Canaanites"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:5",verse:"And I have also heard the groaning of the children of Israel, whom the Egyptians keep in bondage; and I have remembered my covenant.", insight:"God heard the groaning of His people under bondage and remembered His covenant. Your groaning reaches Him too."},
{q:"What does God promise in Exodus 6:6?", options:["To bring Israel out from under the burdens of the Egyptians","To make Moses king of Egypt","To send manna from heaven at once","To part the Red sea that same night"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:6",verse:"Wherefore say unto the children of Israel, I am the LORD, and I will bring you out from under the burdens of the Egyptians, and I will rid you out of their bondage, and I will redeem you with a stretched out arm, and with great judgments:", insight:"'I will bring you out' is the heartbeat of Exodus. God does not merely comfort His people in bondage; He brings them out of it."},
{q:"In Exodus 6:7, what does God promise Israel will become to Him?", options:["His people, and He will be their God","A nation of priests only","The rulers of Egypt","Keepers of Pharaoh's storehouses"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:7",verse:"And I will take you to me for a people, and I will be to you a God: and ye shall know that I am the LORD your God, which bringeth you out from under the burdens of the Egyptians.", insight:"Deliverance ends in belonging: 'I will take you to me for a people.' God saves you into relationship, not just out of trouble."},
{q:"Why did the children of Israel not listen to Moses in Exodus 6:9?", options:["For anguish of spirit and cruel bondage","Because they loved Pharaoh","Because they distrusted Aaron","Because they wanted to stay in Goshen"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:9",verse:"And Moses spake so unto the children of Israel: but they hearkened not unto Moses for anguish of spirit, and for cruel bondage.", insight:"Their pain was so deep they could not hear hope. When someone cannot receive your encouragement, remember it may be the bondage talking, not the heart."},
{q:"What excuse does Moses give in Exodus 6:12 for why Pharaoh will not hear him?", options:["He is of uncircumcised lips","He cannot find the palace","He is afraid of the magicians","He has forgotten God's name"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:12",verse:"And Moses spake before the LORD, saying, Behold, the children of Israel have not hearkened unto me; how then shall Pharaoh hear me, who am of uncircumcised lips?", insight:"Moses points to his faltering speech, but God had already answered that fear. Your weakness is not news to the One who called you."},
{q:"In Exodus 6:13, to whom does the LORD give a charge concerning Israel and Pharaoh?", options:["Moses and Aaron","The elders of Israel","Joshua and Caleb","The magicians of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:13",verse:"And the LORD spake unto Moses and unto Aaron, and gave them a charge unto the children of Israel, and unto Pharaoh king of Egypt, to bring the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt.", insight:"God charges them both: bring out Israel, speak to Pharaoh. The mission is shared, which is why Aaron is there beside Moses."},
{q:"Who is the firstborn son of Israel named at the start of the genealogy in Exodus 6:14?", options:["Reuben","Simeon","Levi","Judah"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:14",verse:"These be the heads of their fathers’ houses: The sons of Reuben the firstborn of Israel; Hanoch, and Pallu, Hezron, and Carmi: these be the families of Reuben.", insight:"The genealogy begins with Reuben, the firstborn, and works down to Levi. God is locating Moses and Aaron inside the family story of Israel."},
{q:"Who are the three sons of Levi named in Exodus 6:16?", options:["Gershon, Kohath, and Merari","Nadab, Abihu, and Eleazar","Amram, Izhar, and Hebron","Libni, Shimi, and Mahli"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:16",verse:"And these are the names of the sons of Levi according to their generations; Gershon, and Kohath, and Merari: and the years of the life of Levi were an hundred thirty and seven years.", insight:"Moses and Aaron come from Kohath's line, the family later set apart to carry the holy things. Their calling ran in the family before they knew it."},
{q:"How many years did Levi live, according to Exodus 6:16?", options:["137 years","133 years","120 years","147 years"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:16",verse:"And these are the names of the sons of Levi according to their generations; Gershon, and Kohath, and Merari: and the years of the life of Levi were an hundred thirty and seven years.", insight:"Levi lived 137 years, long enough to see the family grow toward the promise. God works across generations, not just moments."},
{q:"Whom did Amram take as his wife in Exodus 6:20?", options:["Jochebed","Elisheba","Miriam","Zipporah"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:20",verse:"And Amram took him Jochebed his father’s sister to wife; and she bare him Aaron and Moses: and the years of the life of Amram were an hundred and thirty and seven years.", insight:"Jochebed, the daughter of Levi, is the mother who once hid baby Moses in a basket. Behind the deliverer stands a courageous mother."},
{q:"Who were the sons born to Amram and Jochebed in Exodus 6:20?", options:["Aaron and Moses","Nadab and Abihu","Gershon and Merari","Hur and Caleb"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:20",verse:"And Amram took him Jochebed his father’s sister to wife; and she bare him Aaron and Moses: and the years of the life of Amram were an hundred and thirty and seven years.", insight:"Aaron and Moses, the voice and the leader, born to the same parents. God often pairs different gifts in one family for one mission."},
{q:"How many years did Kohath live, according to Exodus 6:18?", options:["133 years","137 years","110 years","120 years"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:18",verse:"And the sons of Kohath; Amram, and Izhar, and Hebron, and Uzziel: and the years of the life of Kohath were an hundred thirty and three years.", insight:"Kohath lived 133 years, and his son Amram would live 137. The text keeps these numbers to show God's promise outliving every generation that carried it."},
{q:"Who were the sons of Izhar named in Exodus 6:21?", options:["Korah, Nepheg, and Zichri","Mishael, Elzaphan, and Zithri","Libni and Shimi","Mahli and Mushi"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:21",verse:"And the sons of Izhar; Korah, and Nepheg, and Zichri.", insight:"Korah appears here quietly in the genealogy, long before his rebellion in the wilderness. Lineage records even those who will later fall."},
{q:"Whom did Aaron take as his wife in Exodus 6:23?", options:["Elisheba, daughter of Amminadab","Jochebed, daughter of Levi","Zipporah, daughter of Reuel","Miriam, daughter of Amram"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:23",verse:"And Aaron took him Elisheba, daughter of Amminadab, sister of Naashon, to wife; and she bare him Nadab, and Abihu, Eleazar, and Ithamar.", insight:"Aaron married Elisheba of the tribe of Judah, joining the priestly line to the royal tribe. God was weaving the tribes together."},
{q:"Which four sons of Aaron are named in Exodus 6:23?", options:["Nadab, Abihu, Eleazar, and Ithamar","Gershon, Kohath, Merari, and Jochebed","Korah, Dathan, Abiram, and On","Hur, Caleb, Joshua, and Nun"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:23",verse:"And Aaron took him Elisheba, daughter of Amminadab, sister of Naashon, to wife; and she bare him Nadab, and Abihu, Eleazar, and Ithamar.", insight:"These four sons would become Israel's first priests. The genealogy is not trivia; it is the roster of God's coming priesthood."},
{q:"In Exodus 6:26, how are Aaron and Moses described?", options:["The ones to whom the LORD said, Bring out the children of Israel from Egypt","The elders of all the tribes","The keepers of the tabernacle","The scribes of Goshen"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:26",verse:"These are that Aaron and Moses, to whom the LORD said, Bring out the children of Israel from the land of Egypt according to their armies.", insight:"After the whole genealogy, God stamps their identity with one line: these are the ones commanded to bring Israel out. Your calling is your truest name."},
{q:"What does Moses repeat to the LORD in Exodus 6:30?", options:["That he is of uncircumcised lips, so how shall Pharaoh hearken","That he has lost his staff","That Israel has fled to the wilderness","That Aaron refuses to speak"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 6:30",verse:"And Moses said before the LORD, Behold, I am of uncircumcised lips, and how shall Pharaoh hearken unto me?", insight:"Moses ends the chapter with the same fear he brought in. God does not rebuke honest weakness; He answers it with His own sufficiency."}
]},
{n:7, name:"Water Turned to Blood", seal:{ref:"Exodus 7:17",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, In this thou shalt know that I am the LORD: behold, I will smite with the rod that is in mine hand upon the waters which are in the river, and they shall be turned to blood."}, facts:["Did you know the Nile was Egypt's main source of water, food, and trade, so the first plague struck straight at the nation's lifeline? For seven days the river that sustained Egypt could not sustain anyone (Exodus 7:25).","Did you know Aaron's rod swallowed the magicians' rods (Exodus 7:12)? The sign showed that God's power does not merely rival every imitation; it consumes it.","Did you know Moses was 80 and Aaron 83 when they first stood before Pharaoh (Exodus 7:7)? God's deliverance of a nation began with two old men and a staff."], questions:[
{q:"In Exodus 7:1, what does God make Moses to Pharaoh?", options:["A god to Pharaoh","A king over Egypt","A priest of Midian","A scribe of the palace"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:1",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, See, I have made thee a god to Pharaoh: and Aaron thy brother shall be thy prophet.", insight:"God elevates Moses before Pharaoh so the contest is clearly God against Egypt's gods, not man against king. Your authority comes from who sent you."},
{q:"In Exodus 7:1, what role does God give Aaron?", options:["Moses' prophet","Moses' servant","Pharaoh's counselor","Egypt's treasurer"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:1",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, See, I have made thee a god to Pharaoh: and Aaron thy brother shall be thy prophet.", insight:"Aaron is Moses' prophet, the mouthpiece of the message. God pairs the called with the equipped so no one carries the mission alone."},
{q:"How old were Moses and Aaron when they spoke to Pharaoh, according to Exodus 7:7?", options:["Moses 80 and Aaron 83","Moses 70 and Aaron 73","Moses 90 and Aaron 93","Moses 60 and Aaron 63"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:7",verse:"And Moses was fourscore years old, and Aaron fourscore and three years old, when they spake unto Pharaoh.", insight:"Eighty and eighty-three, and just getting started. It is never too late for God to use you for the greatest work of your life."},
{q:"What sign was Moses told to show Pharaoh first, according to Exodus 7:9?", options:["Cast Aaron's rod before Pharaoh, that it become a serpent","Strike the dust of the land","Lift the rod over the river","Call down hail from heaven"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:9",verse:"When Pharaoh shall speak unto you, saying, Shew a miracle for you: then thou shalt say unto Aaron, Take thy rod, and cast it before Pharaoh, and it shall become a serpent.", insight:"The first sign is a preview of the plagues: God's power displayed in Pharaoh's own court. Warning comes before judgment."},
{q:"What does Aaron's rod become before Pharaoh in Exodus 7:10?", options:["A serpent","A sword","A stream of water","A pillar of fire"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:10",verse:"And Moses and Aaron went in unto Pharaoh, and they did so as the LORD had commanded: and Aaron cast down his rod before Pharaoh, and before his servants, and it became a serpent.", insight:"The rod of a shepherd becomes a serpent in the throne room. God turns the ordinary tools of your life into signs of His power."},
{q:"Who did Pharaoh call to imitate the sign of the rod in Exodus 7:11?", options:["The wise men and the sorcerers of Egypt","The priests of Midian","The elders of Israel","The captains of his army"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:11",verse:"Then Pharaoh also called the wise men and the sorcerers: now the magicians of Egypt, they also did in like manner with their enchantments.", insight:"Pharaoh's answer to a miracle is to call the magicians. Counterfeits always appear where the real power of God is at work."},
{q:"What happened to the magicians' rods in Exodus 7:12?", options:["Aaron's rod swallowed them up","They turned to dust","They fled from the palace","Moses broke them in pieces"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:12",verse:"For they cast down every man his rod, and they became serpents: but Aaron’s rod swallowed up their rods.", insight:"Aaron's rod swallowed their rods whole. God's genuine power does not tie with imitation; it devours it."},
{q:"What does God say about Pharaoh's heart in Exodus 7:14?", options:["It is hardened, and he refuses to let the people go","It is softened toward Israel","It is broken in pieces","It is turned to stone"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:14",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Pharaoh’s heart is hardened, he refuseth to let the people go.", insight:"Pharaoh's hardness is named before the first plague falls. A hard heart does not bend at the first sign, which is why the plagues had to be ten."},
{q:"Where was Moses told to meet Pharaoh in the morning in Exodus 7:15?", options:["By the river's brink","At the palace gate","In the temple court","At the city market"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:15",verse:"Get thee unto Pharaoh in the morning; lo, he goeth out unto the water; and thou shalt stand by the river’s brink against he come; and the rod which was turned to a serpent shalt thou take in thine hand.", insight:"God sends Moses to the river at dawn, to the place of Pharaoh's ritual and Egypt's life. Confrontation happens where the idol is strongest."},
{q:"What was Moses told to take in his hand when meeting Pharaoh in Exodus 7:15?", options:["The rod which was turned to a serpent","A bowl of river water","A branch of hyssop","A scroll of the covenant"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:15",verse:"Get thee unto Pharaoh in the morning; lo, he goeth out unto the water; and thou shalt stand by the river’s brink against he come; and the rod which was turned to a serpent shalt thou take in thine hand.", insight:"The same rod, now proven. God reuses the instrument He has already vindicated, reminding Moses and Pharaoh both of what happened last time."},
{q:"In Exodus 7:16, what message was Moses to give Pharaoh?", options:["Let my people go, that they may serve me in the wilderness","Build me a temple in Egypt","Pay tribute to Israel","Come and see the serpent"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:16",verse:"And thou shalt say unto him, The LORD God of the Hebrews hath sent me unto thee, saying, Let my people go, that they may serve me in the wilderness: and, behold, hitherto thou wouldest not hear.", insight:"The demand is worship, not wages: let them go that they may serve God. Freedom's purpose is always devotion."},
{q:"In Exodus 7:17, what does the LORD say will happen to the waters of the river?", options:["They shall be turned to blood","They shall dry up entirely","They shall flood the land","They shall become bitter"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:17",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, In this thou shalt know that I am the LORD: behold, I will smite with the rod that is in mine hand upon the waters which are in the river, and they shall be turned to blood.", insight:"The river that carried baby Moses now carries judgment. The same water that saved the deliverer becomes the first blow against the oppressor."},
{q:"What happened to the fish in the river, according to Exodus 7:18?", options:["They died","They fled to the sea","They turned to stone","They multiplied greatly"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:18",verse:"And the fish that is in the river shall die, and the river shall stink; and the Egyptians shall lothe to drink of the water of the river.", insight:"The fish died, a visible cost of Pharaoh's stubbornness. Refusing God never harms only the one who refuses."},
{q:"How did the river smell after the plague, according to Exodus 7:18?", options:["It stank","It smelled of spices","It smelled of sulfur","It had no smell at all"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:18",verse:"And the fish that is in the river shall die, and the river shall stink; and the Egyptians shall lothe to drink of the water of the river.", insight:"The stinking river was Egypt's pride made repulsive. What a nation worships can become its shame when God judges it."},
{q:"Over which waters was Aaron told to stretch his rod in Exodus 7:19?", options:["The streams, rivers, ponds, and pools of Egypt","Only the river Nile","The wells of Goshen","The canals of the palace"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:19",verse:"And the LORD spake unto Moses, Say unto Aaron, Take thy rod, and stretch out thine hand upon the waters of Egypt, upon their streams, upon their rivers, and upon their ponds, and upon all their pools of water, that they may become blood; and that there may be blood throughout all the land of Egypt, both in vessels of wood, and in vessels of stone.", insight:"No water in Egypt escapes: streams, rivers, ponds, pools. God's judgment is thorough, leaving no refuge for defiance."},
{q:"In Exodus 7:20, what did Moses do to the waters in Pharaoh's sight?", options:["He lifted up the rod and smote the waters of the river","He poured out a bowl of blood","He prayed until the fish died","He commanded the magicians to try first"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:20",verse:"And Moses and Aaron did so, as the LORD commanded; and he lifted up the rod, and smote the waters that were in the river, in the sight of Pharaoh, and in the sight of his servants; and all the waters that were in the river were turned to blood.", insight:"The plague is performed openly, in Pharaoh's sight. God does not hide His works; He wants the king to see exactly whose hand is striking."},
{q:"How did the magicians respond to the plague of blood in Exodus 7:22?", options:["They did the same with their enchantments","They confessed the finger of God","They fled from Egypt","They begged Moses for mercy"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:22",verse:"And the magicians of Egypt did so with their enchantments: and Pharaoh’s heart was hardened, neither did he hearken unto them; as the LORD had said.", insight:"The magicians can copy the plague but cannot cure it. Imitation adds to the misery; only God can lift what He lays down."},
{q:"What did Pharaoh do after seeing the blood, according to Exodus 7:23?", options:["He turned, went into his house, and did not set his heart to it","He freed the Israelites at once","He threw the magicians in prison","He drank the bloodied water"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:23",verse:"And Pharaoh turned and went into his house, neither did he set his heart to this also.", insight:"Pharaoh simply goes home, unmoved. The most dangerous response to God's warning is not anger but indifference."},
{q:"Where did the Egyptians dig for water in Exodus 7:24?", options:["Round about the river","In the wilderness","Under the palace","In the land of Goshen"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:24",verse:"And all the Egyptians digged round about the river for water to drink; for they could not drink of the water of the river.", insight:"All Egypt digs around the dead river for a drink. When God's judgment falls, human effort scrambles for what only He can restore."},
{q:"How many days passed after the river was smitten, according to Exodus 7:25?", options:["Seven days","Three days","Forty days","One day"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 7:25",verse:"And seven days were fulfilled, after that the LORD had smitten the river.", insight:"Seven full days of blood in the water. God gives Pharaoh a complete week to feel the weight and reconsider, and still he will not bend."}
]},
{n:8, name:"Frogs, Lice, and Flies", seal:{ref:"Exodus 8:19",verse:"Then the magicians said unto Pharaoh, This is the finger of God: and Pharaoh’s heart was hardened, and he hearkened not unto them; as the LORD had said."}, facts:["Did you know the magicians could copy the plagues of blood and frogs, but when the dust became lice they failed and admitted, 'This is the finger of God' (Exodus 8:19)? Even Egypt's wise men knew when they were outmatched.","Did you know God set the land of Goshen apart during the plague of flies, so not a single swarm touched the place where Israel lived (Exodus 8:22)? Judgment fell with precision, and His people were marked as His.","Did you know Moses refused Pharaoh's offer to sacrifice 'in the land,' saying the Egyptians would stone them for sacrificing what Egypt held sacred (Exodus 8:26)? True worship could not be negotiated down to Pharaoh's terms."], questions:[
{q:"Where did the frogs come up into, according to Exodus 8:3?", options:["Bedchamber, bed, ovens, and kneadingtroughs","Only the river banks","The palace treasury","The fields of Goshen"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:3",verse:"And the river shall bring forth frogs abundantly, which shall go up and come into thine house, and into thy bedchamber, and upon thy bed, and into the house of thy servants, and upon thy people, and into thine ovens, and into thy kneadingtroughs:", insight:"Frogs in the bed and the bread oven: the plague invades the most private and ordinary places. Disobedience to God leaves no corner of life untouched."},
{q:"Over what was Aaron told to stretch his hand to bring the frogs in Exodus 8:5?", options:["The streams, rivers, and ponds","The dust of the earth","The houses of Pharaoh","The gardens of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:5",verse:"And the LORD spake unto Moses, Say unto Aaron, Stretch forth thine hand with thy rod over the streams, over the rivers, and over the ponds, and cause frogs to come up upon the land of Egypt.", insight:"The frogs come at the stretch of Aaron's hand over the waters. The same waters that bled now swarm, judgment building plague upon plague."},
{q:"What did the magicians do about the frogs in Exodus 8:7?", options:["They brought up frogs upon the land with their enchantments","They killed all the frogs","They hid from Pharaoh","They turned the frogs to dust"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:7",verse:"And the magicians did so with their enchantments, and brought up frogs upon the land of Egypt.", insight:"The magicians can only make the plague worse, never better. Human cleverness without God multiplies problems instead of solving them."},
{q:"What did Pharaoh ask Moses to do in Exodus 8:8?", options:["Entreat the LORD to take away the frogs","Bring more frogs to punish the magicians","Turn the frogs into fish","Leave Egypt at once"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:8",verse:"Then Pharaoh called for Moses and Aaron, and said, Intreat the LORD, that he may take away the frogs from me, and from my people; and I will let the people go, that they may do sacrifice unto the LORD.", insight:"For the first time Pharaoh asks for prayer. Trouble drives even a proud king to beg the God he refused to know."},
{q:"When did Moses say the frogs would be taken away, in Exodus 8:10?", options:["To morrow","This very hour","After seven days","At the new moon"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:10",verse:"And he said, To morrow. And he said, Be it according to thy word: that thou mayest know that there is none like unto the LORD our God.", insight:"'To morrow,' Pharaoh says, choosing one more night with the frogs. Delayed obedience is still disobedience, and it costs another night of misery."},
{q:"Where would the frogs remain after they departed, according to Exodus 8:11?", options:["In the river only","In the palace gardens","In the land of Goshen","In the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:11",verse:"And the frogs shall depart from thee, and from thy houses, and from thy servants, and from thy people; they shall remain in the river only.", insight:"The frogs return to the river, their proper home. God's mercy restores order; the chaos He sends, He can also send back."},
{q:"What happened to the frogs after Moses cried to the LORD in Exodus 8:13?", options:["They died out of the houses, villages, and fields","They returned to the river alive","They turned into lice","They flew away like birds"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:13",verse:"And the LORD did according to the word of Moses; and the frogs died out of the houses, out of the villages, and out of the fields.", insight:"God answers Moses' cry and the frogs die where they are. Answered prayer sometimes leaves a cleanup, but it is still answered prayer."},
{q:"What did Pharaoh do when he saw relief from the frogs in Exodus 8:15?", options:["He hardened his heart and would not listen","He let Israel go","He doubled the Israelites' labor","He fled to the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:15",verse:"But when Pharaoh saw that there was respite, he hardened his heart, and hearkened not unto them; as the LORD had said.", insight:"Relief came, and Pharaoh's heart hardened with it. Blessings received without repentance only strengthen stubbornness."},
{q:"What did the dust of the land become in Exodus 8:16?", options:["Lice throughout all the land of Egypt","Frogs across the fields","Boils on man and beast","Darkness over the land"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:16",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Say unto Aaron, Stretch out thy rod, and smite the dust of the land, that it may become lice throughout all the land of Egypt.", insight:"Dust becomes lice at the smiting of the rod. The ground Egyptians walked on turns against them; creation itself joins God's cause."},
{q:"What happened when the magicians tried to bring forth lice in Exodus 8:18?", options:["They could not","They brought forth twice as many","Their rods were swallowed","The dust turned to blood"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:18",verse:"And the magicians did so with their enchantments to bring forth lice, but they could not: so there were lice upon man, and upon beast.", insight:"The magicians' power runs out at the lice. Every imitation of God has a limit, and here it is reached in the dust."},
{q:"What did the magicians say to Pharaoh in Exodus 8:19?", options:["This is the finger of God","This is the work of Moses","This is a trick of the Hebrews","This is the anger of the river"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:19",verse:"Then the magicians said unto Pharaoh, This is the finger of God: and Pharaoh’s heart was hardened, and he hearkened not unto them; as the LORD had said.", insight:"Egypt's own wise men confess what Pharaoh will not: this is God's finger. Sometimes unbelievers see the truth before the stubborn heart does."},
{q:"What did God say He would sever in Exodus 8:22?", options:["The land of Goshen, where His people dwell, from the swarms of flies","The river from the sea","The palace from the city","The magicians from Pharaoh"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:22",verse:"And I will sever in that day the land of Goshen, in which my people dwell, that no swarms of flies shall be there; to the end thou mayest know that I am the LORD in the midst of the earth.", insight:"For the first time God draws a line: Goshen is severed from the plague. When judgment falls, God knows exactly where His people are."},
{q:"In Exodus 8:23, what did God say He would put between His people and Pharaoh's people?", options:["A division","A wall of fire","A great river","An army of angels"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:23",verse:"And I will put a division between my people and thy people: to morrow shall this sign be.", insight:"God Himself puts the division between His people and Pharaoh's. You do not have to build the wall; belonging to Him is the wall."},
{q:"What filled the houses of the Egyptians in Exodus 8:24?", options:["Swarms of flies","More frogs","A thick darkness","Locusts"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:24",verse:"And the LORD did so; and there came a grievous swarm of flies into the house of Pharaoh, and into his servants’ houses, and into all the land of Egypt: the land was corrupted by reason of the swarm of flies.", insight:"Grievous swarms corrupt the whole land of Egypt. Small creatures, sent by God, bring a nation to its knees."},
{q:"What bargain did Pharaoh offer in Exodus 8:25?", options:["Sacrifice to your God in the land","Take the gold of Egypt and go","Leave your children behind","Serve me one more year"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:25",verse:"And Pharaoh called for Moses and for Aaron, and said, Go ye, sacrifice to your God in the land.", insight:"Pharaoh offers a compromise: worship, but on his terms, in his land. The enemy always prefers a diluted devotion to a departed people."},
{q:"Why did Moses refuse to sacrifice in Egypt, according to Exodus 8:26?", options:["The Egyptians would stone them for sacrificing what Egypt holds sacred","There were no animals left in Egypt","The LORD had forbidden all sacrifice","Pharaoh's priests would steal the offering"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:26",verse:"And Moses said, It is not meet so to do; for we shall sacrifice the abomination of the Egyptians to the LORD our God: lo, shall we sacrifice the abomination of the Egyptians before their eyes, and will they not stone us?", insight:"True worship would have gotten them stoned in Egypt. Some places are spiritually unsafe for obedience, and God calls His people out of them."},
{q:"How far did Moses say they must go to sacrifice in Exodus 8:27?", options:["Three days' journey into the wilderness","To the border of Goshen","Seven days' journey","To the Red sea"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:27",verse:"We will go three days’ journey into the wilderness, and sacrifice to the LORD our God, as he shall command us.", insight:"Three days' journey, as God commanded, no less. Moses will not trim God's command to fit Pharaoh's comfort."},
{q:"What condition did Pharaoh add in Exodus 8:28?", options:["Only ye shall not go very far away","Take Pharaoh's army with you","Leave your flocks behind","Return by the new moon"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:28",verse:"And Pharaoh said, I will let you go, that ye may sacrifice to the LORD your God in the wilderness; only ye shall not go very far away: intreat for me.", insight:"'Do not go very far away': Pharaoh wants them close enough to recall. Halfway obedience keeps you within reach of the old bondage."},
{q:"What happened to the swarms of flies after Moses entreated the LORD in Exodus 8:31?", options:["He removed them, and not one remained","They turned into locusts","They settled only in Goshen","They died but left a stench"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:31",verse:"And the LORD did according to the word of Moses; and he removed the swarms of flies from Pharaoh, from his servants, and from his people; there remained not one.", insight:"Not one fly remained. When God removes a plague, He removes it completely; His mercies are thorough, not partial."},
{q:"What did Pharaoh do at the end of the chapter in Exodus 8:32?", options:["He hardened his heart and would not let the people go","He released Israel with gifts","He imprisoned the magicians","He begged Moses to stay"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 8:32",verse:"And Pharaoh hardened his heart at this time also, neither would he let the people go.", insight:"Three plagues in, and Pharaoh's answer is the same hardened heart. Mercy received without surrender only prepares the heart for greater judgment."}
]},
{n:9, name:"Hail from Heaven", seal:{ref:"Exodus 9:27",verse:"And Pharaoh sent, and called for Moses and Aaron, and said unto them, I have sinned this time: the LORD is righteous, and I and my people are wicked."}, facts:["Did you know the hail of Exodus 9 fell mingled with fire, and the text says nothing like it had been seen in Egypt since it became a nation (Exodus 9:24)? Heaven itself seemed to join the judgment.","Did you know the Egyptians who feared the word of the LORD sheltered their servants and livestock and were spared (Exodus 9:20)? Even in the middle of judgment, listening to God's word was a shelter.","Did you know the hail destroyed the flax and barley but spared the wheat and rye, because they were not yet grown up (Exodus 9:31-32)? God's judgment is measured; even in wrath He leaves a remainder."], questions:[
{q:"What plague did God threaten in Exodus 9:3?", options:["A grievous murrain upon the Egyptian livestock","Frogs in the palace","Darkness for three days","Boils on the magicians"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:3",verse:"Behold, the hand of the LORD is upon thy cattle which is in the field, upon the horses, upon the asses, upon the camels, upon the oxen, and upon the sheep: there shall be a very grievous murrain.", insight:"The murrain strikes Egypt's livestock, the wealth of the nation. God touches what people trust in when lesser warnings are ignored."},
{q:"What did the LORD say He would sever in Exodus 9:4?", options:["The cattle of Israel from the cattle of Egypt","The river from its banks","Goshen from the wilderness","Pharaoh from his throne"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:4",verse:"And the LORD shall sever between the cattle of Israel and the cattle of Egypt: and there shall nothing die of all that is the children’s of Israel.", insight:"Again the severing line: Israel's cattle live, Egypt's die. In judgment God distinguishes His own with perfect accuracy."},
{q:"How many of Israel's cattle died in the murrain, according to Exodus 9:6?", options:["Not one","Half of them","All but the strongest","Only the calves"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:6",verse:"And the LORD did that thing on the morrow, and all the cattle of Egypt died: but of the cattle of the children of Israel died not one.", insight:"Not one of Israel's cattle died. Complete protection is possible in the middle of complete judgment, when you belong to God."},
{q:"What did Pharaoh find when he sent to check Israel's cattle in Exodus 9:7?", options:["Not one of the cattle of the Israelites was dead","They had all been hidden in Goshen","The Israelites had sold them","They were all sick but alive"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:7",verse:"And Pharaoh sent, and, behold, there was not one of the cattle of the Israelites dead. And the heart of Pharaoh was hardened, and he did not let the people go.", insight:"Pharaoh investigates and finds the miracle exactly as Moses said. Evidence stares him in the face, and his heart still does not move."},
{q:"What were Moses and Aaron told to take in Exodus 9:8?", options:["Handfuls of ashes of the furnace","Dust from the riverbank","Water from the Nile","Coals from the altar"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:8",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses and unto Aaron, Take to you handfuls of ashes of the furnace, and let Moses sprinkle it toward the heaven in the sight of Pharaoh.", insight:"Ashes from a furnace, the symbol of Israel's bondage, become the instrument of Egypt's pain. God turns the emblem of suffering into a sign of justice."},
{q:"What did the ashes become in Exodus 9:9?", options:["Boils breaking forth upon man and beast","Lice across the land","A cloud of flies","Hailstones"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:9",verse:"And it shall become small dust in all the land of Egypt, and shall be a boil breaking forth with blains upon man, and upon beast, throughout all the land of Egypt.", insight:"The ashes become boils on skin. This time the plague touches bodies, not just land and water; judgment is getting personal."},
{q:"What happened to the magicians in Exodus 9:11?", options:["They could not stand before Moses because of the boils","They turned the boils to dust","They fled to Goshen","They healed themselves"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:11",verse:"And the magicians could not stand before Moses because of the boils; for the boil was upon the magicians, and upon all the Egyptians.", insight:"The magicians who once copied miracles cannot even stand up now. Opposition to God ends not in debate but in collapse."},
{q:"In Exodus 9:14, what did God say He would send upon Pharaoh's heart?", options:["All His plagues","A sword of judgment","Ten thousand chariots","A famine of seven years"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:14",verse:"For I will at this time send all my plagues upon thine heart, and upon thy servants, and upon thy people; that thou mayest know that there is none like me in all the earth.", insight:"God aims the plagues at Pharaoh's heart, not just his land. The real battlefield was never Egypt; it was the king's stubborn will."},
{q:"Why did God say He had raised Pharaoh up, in Exodus 9:16?", options:["To show His power in him, that His name be declared through all the earth","To make him king forever","To enrich the treasuries of Egypt","To test the patience of Moses"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:16",verse:"And in very deed for this cause have I raised thee up, for to shew in thee my power; and that my name may be declared throughout all the earth.", insight:"Pharaoh's throne exists, in God's purpose, to display God's power to the world. Even a proud king serves a story bigger than himself."},
{q:"What did God promise to rain down in Exodus 9:18?", options:["A very grievous hail, such as had not been in Egypt since its foundation","Fire from heaven alone","A flood like Noah's","Stones from the mountains"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:18",verse:"Behold, to morrow about this time I will cause it to rain a very grievous hail, such as hath not been in Egypt since the foundation thereof even until now.", insight:"God announces the hail a day ahead, giving Egypt time to shelter. Judgment with warning is mercy wearing a stern face."},
{q:"What did those who feared the word of the LORD do in Exodus 9:20?", options:["Made their servants and cattle flee into the houses","Ran to the wilderness","Hid in the pyramids","Offered sacrifices to the river"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:20",verse:"He that feared the word of the LORD among the servants of Pharaoh made his servants and his cattle flee into the houses:", insight:"Some Egyptians believed God's word and acted on it, and they were spared. Faith is proved by what you do before the storm comes."},
{q:"What did those who did not regard the word of the LORD do in Exodus 9:21?", options:["Left their servants and cattle in the field","Mocked Moses openly","Fled to Goshen","Built shelters of stone"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:21",verse:"And he that regarded not the word of the LORD left his servants and his cattle in the field.", insight:"Ignoring God's word cost them their servants and cattle. Disregard for warning is never neutral; it always leaves something in the field."},
{q:"What was mingled with the hail in Exodus 9:24?", options:["Fire","Blood","Dust","Locusts"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:24",verse:"So there was hail, and fire mingled with the hail, very grievous, such as there was none like it in all the land of Egypt since it became a nation.", insight:"Fire running along the ground with the hail: heaven and earth conspiring in one plague. When God judges, all creation obeys."},
{q:"What happened to every man and beast in the field during the hail in Exodus 9:25?", options:["They died","They were blinded","They fled to Goshen","They were turned to stone"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:25",verse:"And the hail smote throughout all the land of Egypt all that was in the field, both man and beast; and the hail smote every herb of the field, and brake every tree of the field.", insight:"The hail kills man and beast alike in the field. The verse is sobering: those who ignored the shelter paid with their lives."},
{q:"Which land had no hail, according to Exodus 9:26?", options:["The land of Goshen, where the children of Israel were","The land of Midian","The palace grounds","The banks of the Nile"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:26",verse:"Only in the land of Goshen, where the children of Israel were, was there no hail.", insight:"Only in Goshen, where Israel dwelt, there was no hail. God's people sat dry while hail hammered Egypt around them."},
{q:"What did Pharaoh confess in Exodus 9:27?", options:["I have sinned this time: the LORD is righteous, and I and my people are wicked","I will serve the LORD forever","The magicians have deceived me","Moses is greater than all gods"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:27",verse:"And Pharaoh sent, and called for Moses and Aaron, and said unto them, I have sinned this time: the LORD is righteous, and I and my people are wicked.", insight:"Pharaoh's confession sounds right, but 'this time' betrays him. True repentance does not schedule its next sin."},
{q:"What did Moses do to stop the thunder and hail in Exodus 9:29?", options:["He spread abroad his hands unto the LORD outside the city","He struck the ground with his rod","He commanded the clouds to part","He covered his head and prayed silently"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:29",verse:"And Moses said unto him, As soon as I am gone out of the city, I will spread abroad my hands unto the LORD; and the thunder shall cease, neither shall there be any more hail; that thou mayest know how that the earth is the LORD’S.", insight:"Moses walks out of the city and spreads his hands to heaven. Intercession sometimes means stepping away from the noise to meet God alone."},
{q:"Which crops were smitten by the hail in Exodus 9:31?", options:["The flax and the barley","The wheat and the rye","The vines and fig trees","The olives and dates"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:31",verse:"And the flax and the barley was smitten: for the barley was in the ear, and the flax was bolled.", insight:"Barley in the ear and flax in bloom were destroyed, the crops at their most vulnerable stage. Timing matters in judgment as in mercy."},
{q:"Why were the wheat and rye not smitten, according to Exodus 9:32?", options:["They were not grown up","They were hidden in Goshen","The hail melted before reaching them","Moses prayed for them"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:32",verse:"But the wheat and the rie were not smitten: for they were not grown up.", insight:"The wheat and rye survived because they were not yet grown. God measures His judgments; even wrath leaves room for what is still to come."},
{q:"What did Pharaoh do when the rain and hail ceased in Exodus 9:34?", options:["He sinned yet more, and hardened his heart","He freed Israel at once","He wept and repented fully","He made Moses a prince"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 9:34",verse:"And when Pharaoh saw that the rain and the hail and the thunders were ceased, he sinned yet more, and hardened his heart, he and his servants.", insight:"The moment the pressure lifts, Pharaoh sins 'yet more.' A heart that only obeys under thunder was never truly surrendered."}
]},
{n:10, name:"Locusts and Darkness", seal:{ref:"Exodus 10:29",verse:"And Moses said, Thou hast spoken well, I will see thy face again no more."}, facts:["Did you know the locusts of Exodus 10 covered the face of the whole earth and ate every green thing the hail had left (Exodus 10:15)? What the hail spared, the locusts finished.","Did you know the darkness over Egypt lasted three days and was so thick it could be felt, yet the children of Israel had light in their dwellings (Exodus 10:22-23)? God's people carried light in the middle of Egypt's night.","Did you know Pharaoh's own servants turned on his stubbornness, pleading, 'Knowest thou not yet that Egypt is destroyed?' (Exodus 10:7)? Even his court could see what the king refused to admit."], questions:[
{q:"Why did God harden Pharaoh's heart, according to Exodus 10:1?", options:["That He might show His signs before him","That Egypt might prosper","That Moses might become king","That the magicians might be honored"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:1",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Go in unto Pharaoh: for I have hardened his heart, and the heart of his servants, that I might shew these my signs before him:", insight:"God's signs are meant to be seen, and Pharaoh's hardness becomes their stage. Even resistance ends up displaying God's power."},
{q:"What question did Moses put to Pharaoh in Exodus 10:3?", options:["How long wilt thou refuse to humble thyself before me?","How long will you sit on the throne?","Will you not fear the river?","When will you pay the tribute?"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:3",verse:"And Moses and Aaron came in unto Pharaoh, and said unto him, Thus saith the LORD God of the Hebrews, How long wilt thou refuse to humble thyself before me? let my people go, that they may serve me.", insight:"The issue was never strength but humility. God waits not for Pharaoh to grow stronger but to bow lower."},
{q:"What did God threaten to bring in Exodus 10:4?", options:["Locusts into Pharaoh's coast","Another hailstorm","A flood of the Nile","An army from the east"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:4",verse:"Else, if thou refuse to let my people go, behold, to morrow will I bring the locusts into thy coast:", insight:"'Tomorrow,' God says through Moses. Warning always precedes the locusts; judgment is never sprung without notice."},
{q:"What would the locusts eat, according to Exodus 10:5?", options:["Every herb of the land, all that the hail had left","The fish of the river","The cattle of Goshen","The fruit of the palace gardens"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:5",verse:"And they shall cover the face of the earth, that one cannot be able to see the earth: and they shall eat the residue of that which is escaped, which remaineth unto you from the hail, and shall eat every tree which groweth for you out of the field:", insight:"The locusts finish what the hail started. Unrepented sin does not stay contained; each judgment consumes what the last one spared."},
{q:"Whose houses would the locusts fill, according to Exodus 10:6?", options:["Pharaoh's, his servants', and all the Egyptians'","Only the houses of the magicians","The houses in Goshen","The royal storehouses"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:6",verse:"And they shall fill thy houses, and the houses of all thy servants, and the houses of all the Egyptians; which neither thy fathers, nor thy fathers’ fathers have seen, since the day that they were upon the earth unto this day. And he turned himself, and went out from Pharaoh.", insight:"No house in Egypt escapes, from palace to hut. When God moves in judgment, status offers no shelter."},
{q:"What did Pharaoh's servants say to him in Exodus 10:7?", options:["How long shall this man be a snare unto us? Knowest thou not yet that Egypt is destroyed?","Let us make war against the Hebrews","The gods of Egypt will save us","Give Moses whatever he asks"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:7",verse:"And Pharaoh’s servants said unto him, How long shall this man be a snare unto us? let the men go, that they may serve the LORD their God: knowest thou not yet that Egypt is destroyed?", insight:"Pharaoh's own servants see the ruin their king denies. Pride blinds the one in charge while everyone around sees clearly."},
{q:"What did Pharaoh ask before letting anyone go in Exodus 10:8?", options:["Who are they that shall go?","How much gold will you take?","When will you return?","Will you leave your God behind?"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:8",verse:"And Moses and Aaron were brought again unto Pharaoh: and he said unto them, Go, serve the LORD your God: but who are they that shall go?", insight:"Pharaoh wants to negotiate the guest list. The enemy loves to discuss terms; God demands full release."},
{q:"Who did Moses say would go, in Exodus 10:9?", options:["Young and old, sons and daughters, flocks and herds","Only the men of war","Only Moses and Aaron","The elders with gifts"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:9",verse:"And Moses said, We will go with our young and with our old, with our sons and with our daughters, with our flocks and with our herds will we go; for we must hold a feast unto the LORD.", insight:"Everyone goes: young and old, sons and daughters, flocks and herds. God's deliverance is never partial; whole families walk out together."},
{q:"What counteroffer did Pharaoh make in Exodus 10:11?", options:["Go now ye that are men, but let your little ones stay","Take all the gold of Egypt","Go but leave your God","Wait until the harvest"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:11",verse:"Not so: go now ye that are men, and serve the LORD; for that ye did desire. And they were driven out from Pharaoh’s presence.", insight:"Pharaoh offers the men while keeping the children as hostages. Compromise with bondage always demands the next generation as its price."},
{q:"What wind brought the locusts in Exodus 10:13?", options:["An east wind","A west wind","A north wind","A whirlwind"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:13",verse:"And Moses stretched forth his rod over the land of Egypt, and the LORD brought an east wind upon the land all that day, and all that night; and when it was morning, the east wind brought the locusts.", insight:"An east wind blows all day and night, then the locusts come. God commands the weather like a servant; nature obeys His judgments."},
{q:"What did the locusts do to the land in Exodus 10:15?", options:["They covered the face of the whole earth and ate every green thing","They burned the fields with fire","They turned the dust to blood","They filled the river with dead fish"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:15",verse:"For they covered the face of the whole earth, so that the land was darkened; and they did eat every herb of the land, and all the fruit of the trees which the hail had left: and there remained not any green thing in the trees, or in the herbs of the field, through all the land of Egypt.", insight:"The land is darkened and stripped bare. Total devastation follows total stubbornness; Pharaoh's 'no' is answered by heaven's 'all.'"},
{q:"What did Pharaoh confess in Exodus 10:16?", options:["I have sinned against the LORD your God, and against you","I will serve the LORD all my days","The servants were right","Moses is a true prophet"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:16",verse:"Then Pharaoh called for Moses and Aaron in haste; and he said, I have sinned against the LORD your God, and against you.", insight:"Pharaoh confesses quickly this time, but haste in confession is not depth in repentance. Words are easy when locusts are eating the land."},
{q:"What did Pharaoh ask Moses to do in Exodus 10:17?", options:["Forgive his sin this once, and entreat the LORD to take away this death","Stay in Egypt as a counselor","Teach the magicians his ways","Divide the locusts among the people"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:17",verse:"Now therefore forgive, I pray thee, my sin only this once, and intreat the LORD your God, that he may take away from me this death only.", insight:"He calls the plague 'this death,' naming it truly at last. Sometimes we only see the weight of our choices when the consequences swarm."},
{q:"What wind took the locusts away in Exodus 10:19?", options:["A mighty strong west wind","A gentle south breeze","A north wind","A whirlwind from the sea"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:19",verse:"And the LORD turned a mighty strong west wind, which took away the locusts, and cast them into the Red sea; there remained not one locust in all the coasts of Egypt.", insight:"The same God who sent the east wind sends a mighty west wind to undo it. The hand that brings judgment is the only hand that can lift it."},
{q:"Where were the locusts cast in Exodus 10:19?", options:["Into the Red sea","Into the wilderness","Into the Nile","Into the pits of Goshen"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:19",verse:"And the LORD turned a mighty strong west wind, which took away the locusts, and cast them into the Red sea; there remained not one locust in all the coasts of Egypt.", insight:"Not one locust remained; all were cast into the sea. When God removes a plague, He removes it utterly, down to the last one."},
{q:"What did Moses say about the livestock in Exodus 10:26?", options:["There shall not an hoof be left behind","Only the lambs may go","The herds will return in a year","Pharaoh may keep the camels"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:26",verse:"Our cattle also shall go with us; there shall not an hoof be left behind; for thereof must we take to serve the LORD our God; and we know not with what we must serve the LORD, until we come thither.", insight:"Not a hoof left behind: Moses refuses to leave any part of Israel's life in Egypt. Partial freedom is still bondage with better terms."},
{q:"What kind of darkness did God send in Exodus 10:21?", options:["Darkness which may be felt","Darkness for forty days","Darkness only at noon","Darkness with thunder"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:21",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Stretch out thine hand toward heaven, that there may be darkness over the land of Egypt, even darkness which may be felt.", insight:"Darkness you can feel is more than absence of light; it is presence of judgment. Egypt, which worshipped the sun, sits blind in the dark."},
{q:"How long did the thick darkness last in Exodus 10:22?", options:["Three days","Seven days","One night","Forty days"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:22",verse:"And Moses stretched forth his hand toward heaven; and there was a thick darkness in all the land of Egypt three days:", insight:"Three days of darkness no one could pierce. God gives Egypt a taste of the grave its king keeps choosing."},
{q:"What did the children of Israel have during the darkness in Exodus 10:23?", options:["Light in their dwellings","Torches in the streets","Fire from heaven","Lamps that never went out"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:23",verse:"They saw not one another, neither rose any from his place for three days: but all the children of Israel had light in their dwellings.", insight:"While Egyptians could not see each other, Israel had light at home. God's people carry His light even when the world around them goes dark."},
{q:"What did Moses say in Exodus 10:29 after Pharaoh threatened him?", options:["Thou hast spoken well, I will see thy face again no more","I will return with a greater plague","Your throne will fall tonight","The LORD will spare you this once"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 10:29",verse:"And Moses said, Thou hast spoken well, I will see thy face again no more.", insight:"Moses accepts Pharaoh's dismissal as final. Some doors close because a hard heart shut them, and the next knock will be the last plague."}
]}
]);
